  "query_preview": "..\n\nSlow progression of governing synoptic features maintains benign \nweather through the weekend and into early next week. The 12z DTX \nraob showed stable conditions and the saturation between 700 and \n550mb responsible for the mostly cloudy day. Satellite imagery \nreveals a vast swath of the central CONUS shrouded in mid-level \ncloud, boosting confidence in a continued mostly cloudy forecast \ngoing forward as this cloud is steered around the SE CONUS 700mb \nridge. A few sprinkles will be possible at times where transient \npockets of forcing emerge within an ill-defined warm frontal \nstructure aloft. The blanket of clouds maintains milder conditions \ntonight, with evening temperatures in the mid 50s settling into the \n40s and lower 50s overnight.\n\nDespite the clouds, positive thickness advection will help boost \nhigh temps back into the 60s Sunday and Monday. A 30% chance for \nlight showers arrives Monday evening and overnight as an upstream \nlow sends a more organized arm of theta-e advection across the Great \nLakes. The bulk of the moisture transport is forecast to hold \nupstream. Arrival of the warm sector Tuesday brings high temps some \n15 to 20 degrees above normal, near 70 degrees.\n\nEvolution of the upstream low exhibits increasing spread beyond \nMonday, but in general a pattern shift is looking likely by mid to \nlate week as a Pacific wave eventually makes its way across the \nGreat Lakes. The wave nearly cuts off from the steering flow as it \ntracks over the Plains Monday, then a northern stream shortwave \ndescends from central Canada on Tuesday. The interaction of these \nwaves opens the door for significant differences to arise wrt timing \nof precip locally. Later solutions that bring rain in late Wednesday \nare driven by a much stronger system that occludes sooner over the \nPlains, delaying arrival of meridional moisture transport. Earlier \nsolutions, which make up a smaller portion of the ensemble \nmembership, appear to result from a slightly stronger system over \nthe Corn Belt that's capable of producing greater poleward moisture \ntransport, but not so strong that the low completely occludes \nupstream. Ultimately, most of the sensitivity in the forecast comes \nfrom the forward speed of the Pacific trough which will come into \nfocus the next few forecast cycles. Highest confidence in precip at \nthis stage is currently on Wednesday, at around 60 to 70%.",
